#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Deploy Brauser's live install from its main checkout.

The live install is the main worktree of this repository: Chrome loads its
extension/dist, the native host manifest points at its target/debug/brauser,
and the LaunchAgent com.rauser.brauser.service runs that binary. This tool
replaces the ad hoc deploy, in order, stopping with a non-zero exit at the
first failure:

  a. check the repo is the main worktree, on main, with no tracked changes
     (untracked files such as EXPLORER/ are fine);
  b. refuse while any Brauser run is queued or running (the service restart
     in step j ends running runs; AGENTS.md), unless --allow-active-runs;
  c. clear stale index.lock files with `worktree-hygiene --locks-only --apply`
     (the same minimum-age, no-holder, no-running-git rules as --clear-locks);
  d. git pull --ff-only; when the pull changes scripts/dev/deploy-live itself,
     re-execute the new copy with the same arguments plus a hidden
     --resumed-from <starting commit>, so every later step (and the summary's
     commit range) is the new code's; a resumed run neither pulls again nor
     re-executes, so the code it runs is always the code it deploys;
  e. check Node can import extension/brand.ts before either build changes a live artifact;
  f. cargo build --locked -p brauser --target-dir <repo>/target/deploy-staging,
     so the live target/debug/brauser is not replaced yet (opening the panel
     starts the live binary, and a new host restarts the service);
  g. npm ci --ignore-scripts, when package-lock.json's sha256 differs from the
     one recorded after the last successful npm ci (in
     <git dir>/deploy-live-state.json, never committed) or node_modules is
     missing; the record is dropped before npm ci starts and written only
     after it succeeds, so an interrupted install runs again next time;
  h. npm run build:extension (replacing extension/dist restarts nothing); a
     build that changed extension/dist records a pending extension reload in
     the same state file, so a deploy refused after this point still prints
     the reload instruction when a later one completes;
  i. check runs again (b): one queued or started during the builds refuses
     here, with the live binary untouched;
  j. publish the staged binary: copy it to a temporary file in target/debug,
     check runs once more, then os.replace it onto target/debug/brauser;
  k. node scripts/register-dev-host.mjs <extension id> <repo>/target/debug/brauser
     (the ID comes from the existing native host manifest's single
     allowed_origins entry unless --extension-id is given);
  l. send the new binary a framed native-messaging hello and check its
     protocol_version and that host_version ends with +<new HEAD, 12 chars>;
  m. print a summary, including a "Reload Brauser" step (the side panel's
     button first, chrome://extensions as the fallback) when extension/dist
     changed in this deploy or a refused one before it, then clear the
     pending reload. It says which extension build Chrome has loaded, from
     the record the host writes on each hello (<support dir>/
     loaded-extension.json, host/src/loaded_extension.rs; brwr-x622):
     "loaded" when that build is extension/dist's, "pending reload" when an
     older build said hello at or after the publish, and "unavailable" when
     no hello since the publish says either, never a guess.

--loaded: only that last report, read-only, for after a reload (the side
panel or settings must open once to say hello); exit 0 loaded, 3 pending
reload, 4 unavailable.

--drain (after step h): pause new runs with a marker the service honours
(host/src/service/drain.rs), wait up to --drain-timeout for queued and
running runs, then go on; the pause is lifted however the deploy ends,
including SIGTERM and SIGHUP (a closed terminal), which exit 128 + the
signal. Only SIGKILL or a crash leaves the marker; the service ignores it an
hour after it was written, or delete <support dir>/deploy-drain.json.
--expected-sha SHA: in step d, fetch origin main and fast-forward exactly to
SHA instead of pulling; refuse a SHA not on origin/main or behind HEAD.
A queued or running run whose service_pid is no live process is stale: it
is reported (with its heartbeat age) and does not block, since its service
is already gone and the next service start recovers it.

Only macOS: the live install is a LaunchAgent and the macOS Chrome
native-messaging manifest; on any other platform it refuses before acting.

--dry-run runs only the read-only checks (a, b, e, the extension ID, the npm ci
decision, the lock step without --apply, and `git ls-remote` for origin
main, so it says what a pull would bring) and prints the rest. Every child runs with
GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0, so this tool's own `git status` calls (and those of the
build scripts it starts) never take the index lock.

Usage:
  scripts/dev/deploy-live [--repo PATH] [--extension-id ID] [--allow-active-runs]
                          [--drain [--drain-timeout SECONDS]] [--expected-sha SHA]
                          [--support-dir PATH] [--native-host-manifest PATH] [--dry-run]
  scripts/dev/deploy-live --loaded [--repo PATH] [--support-dir PATH]
"""

